{"slug":"postgresql","topic":"what","note":"PostgreSQL & Prisma","noteTitle":"PostgreSQL & Prisma","section":"What a relational database is","text":"Everything else in this stack forgets. Restart the server and its memory is empty; close the browser tab and the screen is gone. The database is the part that remembers, and it is the only part where a mistake cannot be fixed by deploying again. In plain English A spreadsheet with rules that are actually enforced. Each sheet is a table with named columns; each row is one thing. The difference from a real spreadsheet is that the database refuses to accept a row that breaks its rules — you cannot type “maybe” into a date column, and you cannot delete a customer who still has orders. Relational means rows point at other rows instead of repeating them. A project stores the id of its owner, not a"},
{"slug":"postgresql","topic":"tables","note":"PostgreSQL & Prisma","noteTitle":"PostgreSQL & Prisma","section":"Tables, keys and constraints","text":"In plain English A table is a spreadsheet with rules that cannot be broken. A column can refuse to be empty, refuse a duplicate, or refuse a value that does not point at a real row in another table. Those rules live in the database rather than in your code, which matters because the database is the one place every piece of code has to go through — including the script somebody runs by hand at midnight. A table is defined by its columns, each with a type that decides what may be stored and what may be done with it. Types are not paperwork: a date stored as text cannot be compared or sorted correctly, and finding that out later means rewriting every row. Type For Note text Any string In Postgr"},
{"slug":"postgresql","topic":"sql","note":"PostgreSQL & Prisma","noteTitle":"PostgreSQL & Prisma","section":"The SQL you will actually write","text":"In plain English SQL reads like a request rather than a procedure. You do not say open the file, loop through it, keep the ones that match — you say which rows you want and the database works out how to get them. That is why the same query can get dramatically faster when you add an index without a single character of the query changing. Four statements cover almost everything. SQL reads like a sentence, which is the whole idea behind it. sql Reading sql Writing ! Run the WHERE as a SELECT first. An UPDATE or DELETE with a mistaken condition is instant and total. Select with the same condition, look at the count, then change the verb. In production, do it inside a transaction so a wrong numb"},
{"slug":"postgresql","topic":"relations","note":"PostgreSQL & Prisma","noteTitle":"PostgreSQL & Prisma","section":"Relations and joins","text":"In plain English Rather than repeating a customer s address on every one of their orders, you store the customer once and have each order point at them. A join is the moment you put those pieces back together to answer a question. Storing each fact once is why correcting an address corrects it everywhere, and joins are the price you pay for that. Three shapes cover essentially every relationship, and picking the wrong one is a schema problem you feel for years. Shape Example here How it is stored One to many A user has many projects The child holds the parent id: projects.user_id. One to one A user has at most one site Same, plus a UNIQUE on the foreign key. Many to many Components belong to"},
{"slug":"postgresql","topic":"indexes","note":"PostgreSQL & Prisma","noteTitle":"PostgreSQL & Prisma","section":"Indexes: why queries are fast or slow","text":"Flow What the database does with your query Replay 1 Parse Is this valid SQL, and do these tables and columns exist? 2 Plan Which indexes exist, how big is each table, which order of joins is cheapest? 3 Execute Read rows — through an index if the plan found one usable, otherwise all of them. 4 Return Send the rows back. EXPLAIN ANALYZE shows every step above with real timings. Without an index, answering “which user has this email” means reading every row. An index is a sorted structure the database maintains so it can jump straight there. In plain English The index at the back of a book. Finding every mention of a word without one means reading the book. The index makes the lookup instant,"},
{"slug":"postgresql","topic":"transactions","note":"PostgreSQL & Prisma","noteTitle":"PostgreSQL & Prisma","section":"Transactions: all of it, or none of it","text":"In plain English Moving money between two accounts is two writes: take from one, add to the other. If the power fails between them, the money has vanished. A transaction wraps both so that either both happen or neither does — there is no moment when the outside world can observe a half-finished operation. A transaction groups several statements so they either all happen or none do. It is the answer to “what if the server dies halfway through”. js The canonical example The guarantees have a well-known acronym, and each letter is a real promise: Means Atomic All of the statements, or none. There is no halfway. Consistent Constraints hold at the end. A transaction cannot leave broken rules behi"},
{"slug":"postgresql","topic":"postgres","note":"PostgreSQL & Prisma","noteTitle":"PostgreSQL & Prisma","section":"What Postgres gives you beyond plain SQL","text":"In plain English Most databases speak roughly the same SQL. This topic is about what Postgres gives you on top of that: proper JSON columns you can search inside, full-text search without running another service, real date and time handling, and extensions — pgvector among them, which is what lets one database serve both your rows and your embeddings. Feature What it is for jsonb Store a whole structure in one column — page content, settings — and still query inside it. This product keeps page structures this way, because their shape is defined by the editor, not by the schema. arrays A list in a column: tags, allowed origins. Simpler than a side table when the list is short and never joined"},
{"slug":"postgresql","topic":"migrations","note":"PostgreSQL & Prisma","noteTitle":"PostgreSQL & Prisma","section":"Changing the schema safely","text":"In plain English Your code changes and the shape of your data has to change with it. A migration is that change written down as a file, committed alongside the code, and applied in the same order everywhere. The alternative — changing the live database by hand — means no two environments match and nobody can say what the current shape actually is. Flow Adding a required column to a live table, without downtime Replay 1 Add it nullable Deploy. Nothing reads it yet, so nothing can break. 2 Write to both New code fills the new column as well as the old one. 3 Backfill in batches Fill existing rows a few thousand at a time, not in one statement that locks the table. 4 Add the constraint Once eve"},
{"slug":"postgresql","topic":"prisma","note":"PostgreSQL & Prisma","noteTitle":"PostgreSQL & Prisma","section":"Prisma: the layer this product actually types","text":"In plain English Prisma sits between your code and the database so queries are written in TypeScript instead of SQL strings, and the compiler knows the shape of what comes back. The real benefit is that a typo in a column name is a build error rather than a runtime one. The real cost is that it hides what SQL is actually being run, which matters the moment something is slow. Prisma is an ORM: you describe your tables once in a schema file, and it generates a typed client. Your editor then knows every model and field, and a typo is a build error instead of a runtime one. prisma prisma/schema.prisma prisma Querying Prisma SQL it becomes findMany SELECT ... FROM ... WHERE ... include A join, or"},
{"slug":"postgresql","topic":"ops","note":"PostgreSQL & Prisma","noteTitle":"PostgreSQL & Prisma","section":"Backups, connections and safety","text":"In plain English The boring half. A backup you have never restored is not a backup — it is a hope. A connection pool sized wrongly takes an application down under exactly the load you built it for. Neither is interesting until the day it is the only thing that matters. Real world The bug that only appears with real data An admin screen lists every user with their project count. In development, with fifty accounts, it answers instantly. In production it times out — because the code fetched all users, then ran one count query per user. Fifty round trips is nothing; fifty thousand is a page nobody can open. The fix is not a faster server. It is one grouped query, or a single Prisma call with th"},
{"slug":"postgresql","topic":"here","note":"PostgreSQL & Prisma","noteTitle":"PostgreSQL & Prisma","section":"The database in this product","text":"In plain English The schema this product actually runs on, and why the tables are shaped the way they are. backend/prisma/schema.prisma — every model, heavily commented. Start at User and follow the relations out. backend/prisma/migrations/ — the history of every schema change, in order. npm run db:studio — a browser view of the development data. The fastest way to understand a schema is to look at real rows in it. npm run db:prod:status — what is pending in production. Safe: it reads. Practice Open db:studio , find your own user row, and follow it to a project. That is a foreign key, in the flesh. In psql or a SQL console, write a query that returns each user’s email and their project count"}
